     "source": "Mata v. Avianca, S.D.N.Y., 22 June 2023; Moffatt v. Air Canada, BC Civil Resolution Tribunal, 14 February 2024; The Register, 6 October 2025.",
     "explain": [
      "These three cases are real, and each one fails in a different way.",
      "**Mata v. Avianca (New York, 2023).** A passenger sued the airline Avianca. His lawyers used ChatGPT to find earlier court decisions that supported his case, and put them in a filing to the court. Six of the decisions didn't exist. One lawyer had even asked ChatGPT whether the cases were real, and it said they were. The judge fined the lawyers and their firm USD 5,000. The lesson: open every source it cites and read it yourself.",
      "**Moffatt v. Air Canada (Canada, 2024).** A customer asked Air Canada's website chatbot about the reduced fare for travel after a death in the family. The chatbot said he could claim it after travelling. The airline's actual policy said otherwise. A tribunal ruled that the airline is responsible for everything on its website, the chatbot included, and ordered it to pay about CAD 812 in damages, interest and fees. The lesson: a customer-facing chatbot speaks for your organisation.",
      "**Deloitte Australia (2025).** Deloitte wrote a report for an Australian government department, for a fee of about AUD 440,000. A researcher found references to academic papers that don't exist and a made-up quote from a court judgment. The report had been drafted with help from a generative AI tool. Deloitte corrected it and paid back the last part of its fee. The lesson: whoever's name is on the cover is responsible for every line, whoever or whatever drafted it.",
      "In all three, the AI did what these tools sometimes do. The real failure was that nobody checked before the work went out."
     ]
